\section{Data Cleaning and Exploratory Data Analysis}

The dataset contains average monthly temperatures for Wellington. To prepare the data for modeling, we first formatted the date index and cast the data into a \texttt{tsibble} object. As per the project requirements, we split the data into a training set (all data up to April 2025) and a test set containing the final $h=12$ months (May 2025 to April 2026) to evaluate our models' true predictive performance. 

We also scanned the series for missing values. Because temperature data is heavily driven by the time of year, simple imputation methods like Last Observation Carried Forward (LOCF) or linear interpolation can distort the natural seasonal curve if a gap occurs during a seasonal peak or trough. To determine the best strategy, we plotted multiple imputation techniques against each other. We selected Seasonal Interpolation (\texttt{na\_seadec}), which decomposes the series, interpolates the seasonally adjusted data, and adds the seasonality back in, preserving the natural climate cycles.

<<data-cleaning, echo=TRUE, fig.cap="Comparison of Missing Value Imputation Methods", fig.pos="H">>=
data <- read_csv("wellington.csv") %>%
  mutate(Date = yearmonth(paste(Year, Month))) %>%
  select(-Month, -Year) %>% 
  as_tsibble(index = Date)

# scanning for missing values
colSums(is.na(data))
scan_gaps(data)

# filling implicit gaps in time index
data <- data %>% fill_gaps()

# convert to ts for imputeTS methods
temp_ts <- ts(data$Temperature, frequency = 12)

# create comparison dataframe with all methods
comparison <- data %>%
  as_tibble() %>%
  mutate(
    Original        = Temperature,
    LOCF            = as.numeric(na.locf(temp_ts, na.rm = FALSE)),
    NOCB            = as.numeric(na.locf(temp_ts, fromLast = TRUE, na.rm = FALSE)),
    Linear          = as.numeric(na_interpolation(temp_ts, option = "linear")),
    Spline          = as.numeric(na_interpolation(temp_ts, option = "spline")),
    Seasonal        = as.numeric(na_seadec(temp_ts, algorithm = "interpolation"))
  ) %>%
  select(Date, Original, LOCF, NOCB, Linear, Spline, Seasonal) %>%
  pivot_longer(-Date, names_to = "Method", values_to = "Temperature")

comparison %>%
  mutate(Method = factor(Method, levels = c("Original", "LOCF", "NOCB",
                                             "Linear", "Spline", "Seasonal"))) %>%
  ggplot(aes(x = Date, y = Temperature)) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 0.3) +
  facet_wrap(~ Method, ncol = 1) +
  labs(title = "Wellington Monthly Temperature — Interpolation Method Comparison",
       y = "Temperature (°C)", x = "Date") +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(strip.text = element_text(face = "bold"))

# apply chosen seasonal imputation
data <- data %>%
  mutate(Temperature = as.numeric(na_seadec(temp_ts, algorithm = "interpolation")))

# creating training + test sets
training <- data %>%
  filter(Date <= yearmonth("2025 April"))

test <- data %>%
  filter(Date > yearmonth("2025 April"))
@

\subsection{Visualizing the Time Series}
To understand the underlying behavior of Wellington's temperatures, we produced a standard time plot, a seasonal subseries plot, and an STL decomposition.

<<eda-plots, echo=TRUE, fig.show="hold", fig.pos="H">>=
# standard time plot
data %>%
  autoplot(Temperature) +
  labs(title = "Wellington Monthly Temperature",
       y = "Temperature (°C)",
       x = "Date") +
  theme_minimal()

# seasonal subseries plot
data %>%
  gg_subseries(Temperature) +
  labs(title = "Wellington Temperature Seasonal Subseries",
       y = "Temperature (°C)",
       x = "Month") +
  theme_minimal()

# STL decomp
data %>%
  model(STL(Temperature ~ trend() + season(window = "periodic"))) %>%
  components() %>%
  autoplot() +
  labs(title = "STL Decomposition of Wellington Temperature") +
  theme_minimal()
@

